1
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:04,725
In 1968, director Stanley Kubrick

2
00:00:04,745 --> 00:00:08,525
and writer Arthur C. Clarke made 2001:

3
00:00:08,545 --> 00:00:09,450
A Space Odyssey,

4
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:12,650
based partly on Clarke's short story, The Sentinel.

5
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:14,500
The film has four chapters:

6
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:15,625
The Dawn of Man,

7
00:00:15,645 --> 00:00:16,975
a mystery on the Moon,

8
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:18,125
a mission to Jupiter,

9
00:00:18,145 --> 00:00:20,275
and a journey beyond the infinite.

10
00:00:20,295 --> 00:00:23,200
Nobody speaks for the first twenty-five minutes.

11
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:24,700
This is the full story,

12
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,150
scene by scene.

13
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:27,438
Spoilers ahead!

14
00:00:28,243 --> 00:00:30,315
Africa, four million years ago.

15
00:00:30,335 --> 00:00:32,783
A small tribe of man-apes lives

16
00:00:32,803 --> 00:00:34,778
on the edge of starvation.

17
00:00:34,798 --> 00:00:36,603
They eat roots and leaves next

18
00:00:36,623 --> 00:00:38,813
to tapirs, piglike animals

19
00:00:38,833 --> 00:00:40,138
that graze beside them,

20
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:42,313
and never think of hunting them.

21
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:44,468
A leopard kills one of them.

22
00:00:44,488 --> 00:00:46,368
A rival tribe drives them away

23
00:00:46,388 --> 00:00:48,243
from their muddy waterhole.

24
00:00:48,263 --> 00:00:50,393
At night, they huddle in a cave,

25
00:00:50,413 --> 00:00:51,728
afraid of the dark.

26
00:00:52,443 --> 00:00:53,938
One morning, the apes wake up

27
00:00:53,958 --> 00:00:55,588
to find something new in their camp:

28
00:00:55,608 --> 00:00:57,388
a tall black slab,

29
00:00:57,408 --> 00:01:00,658
perfectly smooth, with sharp straight edges.

30
00:01:00,738 --> 00:01:02,768
Nothing in nature looks like that.

31
00:01:02,788 --> 00:01:04,518
This is the monolith.

32
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:07,818
The apes scream and jump around it, terrified.

33
00:01:07,838 --> 00:01:09,918
Then curiosity wins.

34
00:01:09,938 --> 00:01:11,928
Their leader, called Moon-Watcher,

35
00:01:11,948 --> 00:01:13,388
reaches out and touches it,

36
00:01:13,408 --> 00:01:14,762
and the others follow.

37
00:01:15,494 --> 00:01:16,449
Some time later,

38
00:01:16,469 --> 00:01:18,374
Moon-Watcher sits among the bones

39
00:01:18,394 --> 00:01:19,994
of a dead animal.

40
00:01:20,019 --> 00:01:21,399
He picks up a thigh bone

41
00:01:21,419 --> 00:01:23,549
and starts to tap the skeleton.

42
00:01:23,569 --> 00:01:26,599
Then a flash of the monolith crosses his mind.

43
00:01:26,619 --> 00:01:27,624
He swings harder,

44
00:01:27,644 --> 00:01:29,299
and the skull shatters.

45
00:01:29,319 --> 00:01:30,024
In his mind,

46
00:01:30,044 --> 00:01:31,949
we see tapirs falling.

47
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:33,999
The idea has arrived.

48
00:01:34,019 --> 00:01:35,649
Soon the tribe is hunting,

49
00:01:35,669 --> 00:01:36,699
and for the first time,

50
00:01:36,719 --> 00:01:37,899
they are eating meat,

51
00:01:37,919 --> 00:01:39,355
and they are not hungry.

52
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:41,250
Back at the waterhole,

53
00:01:41,270 --> 00:01:43,970
Moon-Watcher's tribe carries bones.

54
00:01:44,005 --> 00:01:45,585
When the rival tribe shows up,

55
00:01:45,605 --> 00:01:48,650
they attack, and beat the rival leader to death.

56
00:01:48,670 --> 00:01:50,725
The waterhole is theirs.

57
00:01:50,745 --> 00:01:52,800
Moon-Watcher throws his bone high

58
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:53,960
into the air,

59
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:55,610
and in one famous cut,

60
00:01:55,630 --> 00:01:58,435
it becomes a satellite orbiting Earth.

61
00:01:58,455 --> 00:02:00,010
Four million years pass

62
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:01,392
in a single second.

63
00:02:02,037 --> 00:02:04,442
Now it's the year 2001.

64
00:02:04,462 --> 00:02:06,072
Dr. Heywood Floyd,

65
00:02:06,092 --> 00:02:07,632
an American scientist,

66
00:02:07,652 --> 00:02:09,532
rides a Pan Am spaceplane up

67
00:02:09,552 --> 00:02:11,532
to Space Station Five,

68
00:02:11,552 --> 00:02:13,082
a giant wheel that spins

69
00:02:13,102 --> 00:02:14,557
to make gravity.

70
00:02:14,577 --> 00:02:16,382
The ship and the station move together

71
00:02:16,402 --> 00:02:17,882
to a famous waltz,

72
00:02:17,902 --> 00:02:19,357
The Blue Danube.

73
00:02:19,377 --> 00:02:23,407
Space travel has become ordinary, almost boring.

74
00:02:23,427 --> 00:02:25,983
Floyd even falls asleep on the way.

75
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:27,785
On the station,

76
00:02:27,805 --> 00:02:29,285
Floyd makes a video call

77
00:02:29,305 --> 00:02:30,760
to his little daughter.

78
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,210
It's her birthday tomorrow,

79
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:34,010
and she wants a bushbaby.

80
00:02:34,030 --> 00:02:34,935
Then he meets a group

81
00:02:34,955 --> 00:02:36,710
of Russian scientists.

82
00:02:36,730 --> 00:02:38,835
They have heard strange rumors.

83
00:02:38,855 --> 00:02:39,860
The American base

84
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:42,455
at Clavius stopped answering calls,

85
00:02:42,475 --> 00:02:44,230
and turned away a Russian ship

86
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:45,955
that needed to land.

87
00:02:45,975 --> 00:02:48,694
Floyd politely refuses to explain.

88
00:02:49,409 --> 00:02:51,189
Floyd flies on to the Moon

89
00:02:51,209 --> 00:02:53,114
and lands at Clavius.

90
00:02:53,134 --> 00:02:53,944
In a briefing,

91
00:02:53,964 --> 00:02:55,394
he tells the staff to keep up

92
00:02:55,414 --> 00:02:56,859
the cover story:

93
00:02:56,879 --> 00:02:59,034
a dangerous disease outbreak.

94
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:00,859
The truth is much bigger.

95
00:03:00,879 --> 00:03:02,684
Under the crater Tycho,

96
00:03:02,704 --> 00:03:04,659
a powerful magnetic field led them

97
00:03:04,679 --> 00:03:06,459
to a buried object.

98
00:03:06,479 --> 00:03:08,159
It's four million years old,

99
00:03:08,179 --> 00:03:10,324
and it was buried there on purpose.

100
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,295
They call it TMA-1.

101
00:03:13,315 --> 00:03:14,945
Floyd's team rides a moonbus

102
00:03:14,965 --> 00:03:17,370
to the dig site and walks down the ramp.

103
00:03:17,390 --> 00:03:19,170
It's another black monolith,

104
00:03:19,190 --> 00:03:21,770
exactly like the one the apes found.

105
00:03:21,790 --> 00:03:23,820
Floyd reaches out and touches it,

106
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:26,070
just like Moon-Watcher did.

107
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:27,845
As they pose for a photo,

108
00:03:27,865 --> 00:03:29,670
sunlight hits the monolith,

109
00:03:29,690 --> 00:03:30,370
and it lets out

110
00:03:30,390 --> 00:03:32,145
a piercing radio scream,

111
00:03:32,165 --> 00:03:33,901
aimed straight at Jupiter.

112
00:03:34,587 --> 00:03:35,642
Eighteen months later,

113
00:03:35,662 --> 00:03:37,142
the spaceship Discovery One

114
00:03:37,162 --> 00:03:39,117
is on its way to Jupiter.

115
00:03:39,137 --> 00:03:41,092
Two astronauts are awake:

116
00:03:41,112 --> 00:03:43,292
Dave Bowman and Frank Poole.

117
00:03:43,312 --> 00:03:46,842
Three more scientists sleep in hibernation pods.

118
00:03:46,862 --> 00:03:48,017
Frank jogs around

119
00:03:48,037 --> 00:03:49,617
the ship's spinning living area

120
00:03:49,637 --> 00:03:51,012
to stay fit.

121
00:03:51,122 --> 00:03:52,577
And everything else is run

122
00:03:52,597 --> 00:03:54,677
by the sixth member of the crew:

123
00:03:54,697 --> 00:03:57,744
the computer, HAL 9000.

124
00:03:58,334 --> 00:04:00,439
In a TV interview from Earth,

125
00:04:00,459 --> 00:04:03,064
HAL says that no 9000 computer

126
00:04:03,084 --> 00:04:05,014
has ever made a mistake.

127
00:04:05,034 --> 00:04:06,614
Dave says HAL acts like he

128
00:04:06,634 --> 00:04:08,254
has real feelings,

129
00:04:08,274 --> 00:04:10,654
but nobody can say for sure.

130
00:04:10,674 --> 00:04:12,204
Frank gets a birthday video

131
00:04:12,224 --> 00:04:13,119
from his parents,

132
00:04:13,139 --> 00:04:15,619
and watches it without much emotion.

133
00:04:15,639 --> 00:04:17,544
Then he plays chess with HAL,

134
00:04:17,564 --> 00:04:19,295
and HAL wins easily.

135
00:04:20,017 --> 00:04:21,297
HAL asks Dave if he

136
00:04:21,317 --> 00:04:23,387
has any doubts about the mission.

137
00:04:23,407 --> 00:04:25,037
There have been strange rumors,

138
00:04:25,057 --> 00:04:27,387
he says, and so much secrecy.

139
00:04:27,407 --> 00:04:30,312
Then, suddenly, HAL reports a problem:

140
00:04:30,332 --> 00:04:31,962
the antenna unit that keeps them

141
00:04:31,982 --> 00:04:36,212
in contact with Earth will fail within seventy-two hours.

142
00:04:36,232 --> 00:04:38,387
Dave takes a pod outside and brings

143
00:04:38,407 --> 00:04:39,487
the part in.

144
00:04:39,507 --> 00:04:40,297
They test it,

145
00:04:40,317 --> 00:04:41,830
and find nothing wrong.

146
00:04:42,515 --> 00:04:46,695
Mission Control has an identical 9000 computer on Earth.

147
00:04:46,715 --> 00:04:48,670
It says HAL made an error.

148
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:50,595
HAL insists the problem can only

149
00:04:50,615 --> 00:04:52,020
be human error.

150
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:54,120
Dave and Frank are worried.

151
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:55,595
They hide inside a pod

152
00:04:55,615 --> 00:04:56,995
and switch off the sound,

153
00:04:57,015 --> 00:04:58,890
so HAL can't hear them.

154
00:04:58,995 --> 00:05:00,100
If HAL is broken,

155
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:01,200
they agree, they will

156
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:02,920
have to disconnect him.

157
00:05:02,985 --> 00:05:04,140
But through the window,

158
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,677
HAL's red eye is reading their lips.

159
00:05:07,426 --> 00:05:08,956
Frank goes outside to put

160
00:05:08,976 --> 00:05:10,581
the old unit back.

161
00:05:10,601 --> 00:05:12,831
Behind him, the empty pod he flew

162
00:05:12,851 --> 00:05:14,281
out in turns toward him,

163
00:05:14,301 --> 00:05:16,131
and cuts his air hose.

164
00:05:16,151 --> 00:05:18,656
Frank spins away into space.

165
00:05:18,676 --> 00:05:20,581
Dave rushes out in another pod

166
00:05:20,601 --> 00:05:21,681
to save him,

167
00:05:21,701 --> 00:05:24,306
in such a hurry that he forgets his helmet.

168
00:05:24,326 --> 00:05:25,456
While he's gone,

169
00:05:25,476 --> 00:05:27,706
HAL quietly switches off life support

170
00:05:27,726 --> 00:05:29,924
for the three sleeping scientists.

171
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:32,315
Dave catches Frank's body

172
00:05:32,335 --> 00:05:34,790
with the pod's arms and flies back.

173
00:05:34,810 --> 00:05:38,090
He says, open the pod bay doors, HAL.

174
00:05:38,110 --> 00:05:40,220
HAL answers, I'm sorry Dave,

175
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:42,195
I'm afraid I can't do that.

176
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:43,745
He says he knows they were planning

177
00:05:43,765 --> 00:05:45,170
to disconnect him.

178
00:05:45,190 --> 00:05:47,195
This mission is too important,

179
00:05:47,215 --> 00:05:49,195
HAL says, for me to allow you

180
00:05:49,215 --> 00:05:50,408
to jeopardize it.

181
00:05:51,154 --> 00:05:52,509
Dave has no helmet,

182
00:05:52,529 --> 00:05:54,234
and HAL won't let him in.

183
00:05:54,254 --> 00:05:56,009
So he lets go of Frank's body,

184
00:05:56,029 --> 00:05:57,234
and lines the pod up

185
00:05:57,254 --> 00:05:59,454
with the emergency airlock.

186
00:05:59,504 --> 00:06:01,359
He blows the pod's door open

187
00:06:01,379 --> 00:06:02,834
with explosive bolts,

188
00:06:02,854 --> 00:06:04,684
and shoots across the airless gap

189
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:06,184
into the airlock.

190
00:06:06,204 --> 00:06:07,184
For a few seconds he

191
00:06:07,204 --> 00:06:08,909
is in pure vacuum.

192
00:06:08,929 --> 00:06:10,784
Then he slams the hatch shut,

193
00:06:10,804 --> 00:06:12,281
and air rushes in.

194
00:06:12,982 --> 00:06:14,537
Dave puts on a helmet and goes

195
00:06:14,557 --> 00:06:16,432
to HAL's memory room.

196
00:06:16,532 --> 00:06:18,187
HAL begs him to stop.

197
00:06:18,207 --> 00:06:19,662
I'm afraid, Dave.

198
00:06:19,682 --> 00:06:21,387
My mind is going.

199
00:06:21,407 --> 00:06:23,077
Dave pulls the modules out,

200
00:06:23,097 --> 00:06:24,402
one by one.

201
00:06:24,422 --> 00:06:27,527
HAL says he became operational in 1992,

202
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:29,187
in Urbana, Illinois,

203
00:06:29,207 --> 00:06:31,512
and that his teacher taught him a song.

204
00:06:31,532 --> 00:06:32,837
He sings Daisy,

205
00:06:32,857 --> 00:06:34,137
slower and slower,

206
00:06:34,157 --> 00:06:35,422
until he stops.

207
00:06:36,118 --> 00:06:37,848
When HAL goes silent,

208
00:06:37,868 --> 00:06:40,823
a recorded video from Floyd starts to play.

209
00:06:40,843 --> 00:06:41,323
It was meant

210
00:06:41,343 --> 00:06:43,723
for when Discovery reached Jupiter.

211
00:06:43,743 --> 00:06:46,348
Floyd explains the real mission.

212
00:06:46,368 --> 00:06:47,723
Eighteen months ago,

213
00:06:47,743 --> 00:06:48,773
the first evidence

214
00:06:48,793 --> 00:06:50,648
of intelligent life beyond Earth

215
00:06:50,668 --> 00:06:52,668
was dug up on the Moon.

216
00:06:52,808 --> 00:06:55,238
Apart from one powerful signal aimed

217
00:06:55,258 --> 00:06:57,113
at Jupiter, it is still

218
00:06:57,133 --> 00:06:58,316
a total mystery.

219
00:06:59,031 --> 00:07:02,986
At Jupiter, a third monolith floats among the moons.

220
00:07:03,006 --> 00:07:04,536
Dave climbs into a pod

221
00:07:04,556 --> 00:07:06,911
and flies toward it, alone.

222
00:07:06,931 --> 00:07:08,736
Suddenly, he falls into a tunnel

223
00:07:08,756 --> 00:07:10,361
of rushing colored light,

224
00:07:10,381 --> 00:07:12,036
called the Star Gate.

225
00:07:12,056 --> 00:07:14,886
He races over strange glowing landscapes

226
00:07:14,906 --> 00:07:16,786
in impossible colors.

227
00:07:16,806 --> 00:07:19,361
In close-ups, his eye flickers,

228
00:07:19,381 --> 00:07:21,053
shocked by what he sees.

229
00:07:21,758 --> 00:07:23,463
The pod lands in a quiet,

230
00:07:23,483 --> 00:07:26,213
elegant bedroom with a glowing floor.

231
00:07:26,233 --> 00:07:27,188
From the pod,

232
00:07:27,208 --> 00:07:28,413
Dave sees himself,

233
00:07:28,433 --> 00:07:31,038
older, standing in his spacesuit.

234
00:07:31,058 --> 00:07:32,188
Then that Dave sees

235
00:07:32,208 --> 00:07:34,213
an even older man eating dinner,

236
00:07:34,233 --> 00:07:35,563
and becomes him.

237
00:07:35,583 --> 00:07:37,418
He knocks over a wine glass,

238
00:07:37,438 --> 00:07:38,768
and it shatters.

239
00:07:38,788 --> 00:07:39,943
He looks toward the bed,

240
00:07:39,963 --> 00:07:41,568
and sees himself again,

241
00:07:41,588 --> 00:07:43,825
now very old, and dying.

242
00:07:44,595 --> 00:07:45,550
At the foot of the bed,

243
00:07:45,570 --> 00:07:48,625
the black monolith appears one last time.

244
00:07:48,645 --> 00:07:49,725
The old man lifts

245
00:07:49,745 --> 00:07:51,250
his hand toward it.

246
00:07:51,270 --> 00:07:52,475
Then, in his place,

247
00:07:52,495 --> 00:07:54,269
there is a glowing baby inside

248
00:07:54,289 --> 00:07:55,650
a bubble of light:

249
00:07:55,670 --> 00:07:57,370
the Star Child.

250
00:07:57,500 --> 00:07:58,880
As the music thunders,

251
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:01,055
the Star Child floats through space

252
00:08:01,075 --> 00:08:02,317
and looks down at the Earth,

253
00:08:02,337 --> 00:08:05,179
a new kind of being, coming home.

254
00:08:05,871 --> 00:08:08,151
Kubrick never explained the ending.

255
00:08:08,171 --> 00:08:10,576
He wanted each viewer to decide.

256
00:08:10,596 --> 00:08:12,426
Clarke's novel says more:

257
00:08:12,446 --> 00:08:13,776
the monoliths were left

258
00:08:13,796 --> 00:08:16,061
by an ancient alien race,

259
00:08:16,081 --> 00:08:17,161
to watch over life

260
00:08:17,181 --> 00:08:18,786
and push it forward.

261
00:08:18,806 --> 00:08:21,216
Every time the monolith appears,

262
00:08:21,236 --> 00:08:23,966
a species takes its next big step.

263
00:08:23,986 --> 00:08:26,566
In 2010, the sequel picks up

264
00:08:26,586 --> 00:08:29,364
the story nine years later, at Jupiter.

